From 529cc76e4bc3b72548828f15a12d1d5f52986064 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: local <local@example.invalid>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 00:19:57 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 30/34] WP18: Prepare a later Postgres move

Integration: db.py conflicts with WP06, WP13, WP14 and WP22. Kept WP06's startup_lock next to the migration runner. db.execute() follows WP18 (returns the row count) but runs on WP14's per-thread connection through _helper_conn(), so WP13's counters still apply; WP14's execute_rowcount() is kept as an alias and its _inserts() helper is gone. db.insert() runs its INSERT ... RETURNING id on _helper_conn() instead of a private connect(). Kept WP22's invoices.expired_ids query (already null-safe without IS ?), WP12's lifecycle_mail_sent delete in retention._delete_workspace with plain = for the audit delete, and WP14's execute_rowcount call in routes/admin.py and WP04's audit filter, both with db.null_safe_eq. AGENTS.md keeps both new sections (WP17 copy rules, WP18 database access). WP12, WP15, WP16 and WP23 columns precede WP18 in this series and are part of the frozen baseline. Updated tests/test_connection_reuse.py (WP14) for the row-count contract.

Conflict resolution: db._init_db_locked: kept PR 230's _resolve_legacy_resign_alerts(conn) right after _reset_reverted_stay_fee, before WP18 records the baseline and applies the numbered migrations; PR 230's submission.retried_at column is part of the frozen baseline. Cross-WP fix for PR 230: workspace_export.py's stay-fee filing query (the ZIP now carries the filings) used a bare owner_user_id IS ?, which WP18's portability guard rejects; it now uses db.null_safe_eq like the other export queries.
